Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Hierarchical Clustering", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.833934793044127*^9, 
  3.833934798773909*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1ac03a7a-2a30-4937-ac80-\
5663848b31db"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "One can load the package \
Needs[\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]HierarchicalClustering`\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]] \
and then have access to functions like Agglomerate, which re-order the rows \
of a matrix by hierarchical clustering, and ClusterFlatten, which flatten \
those clusterings down to a one-dimensional list.  For example, if you have a \
symmetrical correlation matrix (for example a correlation matrix you adjusted \
using AdjustCorrelMatpac, and then restored to symmetry with RestoreSymmetry, \
then you could use this code\n\n",
 StyleBox["posnlist",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "=With[{mat= Normal[RestoreSymmetry[",
 StyleBox["matrix",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "]]\[Rule]Range[Length[",
 StyleBox["matrix",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "]]},\n\tClusterFlatten[Agglomerate[",
 StyleBox["mat",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ",Linkage\[Rule]\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]Ward\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]]]]\n\t\n\
and the output would be a list of row positions, representing a re-ordering \
of the rows that fits the clustering.  \n\nOne can then define an array = ",
 StyleBox["matrix",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\[LeftDoubleBracket]",
 StyleBox["posnlist",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["posnlist",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\[RightDoubleBracket]], and simply plot it using ArrayPlot and an \
appropriate Color Function that deals well with values that are mostly \
between -0.5 and 0.5.  \n\nHowever it is very difficult, if the array is \
large, to label it in a manner that\[CloseCurlyQuote]s readilble.  "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.833934812287764*^9, 
  3.833935256231987*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"42bc17d5-e73b-41df-85c7-\
19254ef94ae7"],

